A||||5||Zoom88||E-brake - Rear brakes, whats the relation !!||||1||
Z||000000||Zoom88||12-26-2001||12:00 PM||||<BR>  I keep hearing that the brakes on the 88's are suppose to be good. Mine fall far short from even fair !! I believe I read somewhere (of course can't find it now) that the rear brakes somehow adjust through the E-Brake or Emergency Brake. If so how does this work and if the E-Brake is not working correctly how could one adjust the rear brakes?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||12-26-2001||12:36 PM||||Well, the rear caliper pistons have a mechanical "screw drive" in them for the emergency brake system.  This drive will only allow the piston to be bottomed into the caliper a small amount when you take your foot off the brake.  The screw drive system in it keeps the piston out, and is what puts the brakes on for the e-brake.  <P>There isn't much of a way to do a self adjustment on it.  I'd sugegst first getting the e-brake system working if its not by adjusting the cables.  Then if the thing still isn't working you might need to do a rebuild of the rear calipers.<P>If you think the brakes are that bad on the car, you might want to rebuild/replace all the calipers anyway.  Replacing the rubber brake lines with steel braded lines wouldn't hurt either.<P>I just finished rebuilding the front calipers on my 88 Formula and have noticed a world of difference.  I never thought the brakes on the car were really better than the 84-87's, but they just weren't working well because of age.  Now that the calipers have been rebuilt the brakes are a lot better.  They still aren't the best brakes in the world but there is a noticeable improvement over the older units.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PAUL88GT||12-26-2001||07:20 PM||||The brakes on my 88GT are noticeably better than the brakes on my 86SE.  I just replaced the rear caliper on the 86SE.  Mine was leaking a little.  It is kind of a pain on the rear.  I had take the tension out of the e-brake cable so I could get it removed from the caliper.  Brakes are always a messy job.  My guess is there is something wrong with your calipers. Those 88 brakes should be smooth.  Good luck<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||12-27-2001||12:59 AM||||The parking brake should be working to keep the rear brakes adjusted. It will help alot. Use the e-brake often to keep the rears adjusted. If it doesn't work, fix it.<P>BTW, I owned an 86SE V6 for about 6 years before I got my 88. I will never own a pre 88 again! Even after I just shelled out 120 bucks for a reman rear caliper!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||12-27-2001||01:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SCCA FIERO:<BR><B>The parking brake should be working to keep the rear brakes adjusted. It will help alot. Use the e-brake often to keep the rears adjusted. If it doesn't work, fix it.<P>BTW, I owned an 86SE V6 for about 6 years before I got my 88. I will never own a pre 88 again! Even after I just shelled out 120 bucks for a reman rear caliper!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>YES!  Another 88 convert!  Let us all repent and drive 88's!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000005||JohnF||12-27-2001||05:41 PM||||As another '88 driver  - I recently did a normal brake job all-around (new pucks, turn rotors, etc.).  '88 brakes may not be the best in the world but they stop me plenty well (e-brake works fine also).<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://photos.msn.com/ImageServer/Image.aspx?Image=0h9xCkj4Yyo6VbTdcNrmhglJSkAWB6C91volMeDlp0h*oii8TPGa20kyOxpdcqzP8qgFSFmsURSXXLzrLUqyll5ofimdP92*FNFuzBBDM1PEajQxQ*Q8WWq*rfvz6!x4pUtE2EsH24ClDyP3oWXL*H41TkaSbtSsDJGlSZ4dol6UKjqoNXe9R!uqr1PsTEkyvCkblQOE!Ntm6y7ZIR0Yh4dj5E3iRUs!aOGI2F9kotBxoa!4otRtSuGucZ1Pen9q24d0hrglVvzK*EY4KHAosZ4!AMC1kyuNFUR*urYcA*t61aKP0zREsXUVoyq0!cXPxGXJFnyglKwpMClTPrmLCQv*qNBY9KpHMNz9*AOylphEbbQcqGuVPQ$$"> <P>You can have all the SPEED you can afford!||||reg||10
